
\section{Methodology and Architecture}
\label{sec:methodology}

\subsection{Scope}

The subject of this section is a single component of the wider
study: the biofeedback engine that runs on the lab workstation,
listens to the participant's physiology, and drives the VR scene in
response. Everything below explains what that engine does and why
it does it that way.

Three neighbouring topics are handled by other sections of the
paper. The VR scenes themselves
(balloon flight for acrophobia, spider dynamics for arachnophobia,
audience behaviour for public speaking) are the responsibility of
the Unity team, and a placeholder subsection later in this
methodology (Section~\ref{sec:unity-placeholder}) marks where their
content slots into the paper. Study-level ethics (approval numbers,
consent language, retention policy) belong to the general study
description. Where those neighbouring topics matter here they are
mentioned briefly and then handed off.


% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{Framing}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

Virtual reality exposure therapy for phobias has been established
across three decades of clinical work
\citep{rothbaum1995, emmelkamp2002, wiederhold2005}, and later
meta-analyses have shown it non-inferior to in-vivo
exposure~\citep{powers2008}. Recent reviews collect the state of
the art \citep{botella2017}, and randomised controlled trials of
fully automated VR exposure now report clinically significant
reductions in fear-of-heights scores~\citep{freeman2018}. The
approach works. What limits its reach at present is not efficacy
but engineering.

One design decision shaped everything else in our framework.
Physiological measurement, per-participant baselining, stress
computation, and VR presentation are four separate problems.
Systems reported in the adaptive VR literature ,
\citet{moldoveanu2023} for acrophobia, \citet{garciapalacios2002}
for spider phobia, \citet{anderson2013} for social anxiety ,
typically bundle all four inside one codebase that is tightly
coupled to one scene. Every new phobia then means duplicating the
physiology stack.

We deliberately separated the layers. Our engine is a server. Each
VR phobia scene is a client. Two network channels connect them:
short text commands travel from server to Unity, structured JSON
telemetry travels from Unity back to the server. Neither channel
knows or cares which scene is loaded. Adding a fourth phobia is
Unity-side work only.

Physiologically we do not claim novelty. The extraction chain and
the weighted composite come directly from \citet{moldoveanu2023}.
What is new here is the scene-agnostic infrastructure that lets
that same chain support a range of exposure paradigms in one lab
on one set of sensors. Standardising the measurement side across
studies reduces cost, makes cross-phobia comparisons meaningful,
and shortens the path from ``we want a new exposure paradigm'' to
``we have a working prototype''.

An earlier study conducted at this lab, reported by
\citet{daghooghi}, ran a similar VR exposure protocol without any
biofeedback loop. The framework described here is the technical
extension that closes that loop. Continuity between the two
versions of the study matters for how the paper reads.

% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{Modularity as a design principle}
\label{sec:modularity}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

Every important component in the pipeline is swappable at a single
edit point. Modularity is not decoration here; it is the property
that lets one lab support multiple phobias, run offline replays
alongside live sessions, and compare independent signal-processing
libraries against each other on the same data. Four axes of
modularity matter.

\textbf{Data source.} A configuration flag switches between two
data sources without touching any downstream code. In
\texttt{real\_plux} mode the engine subscribes to the live PLUX
device through OpenSignals. In \texttt{mock} mode it replays a
recorded OpenSignals file at native rate. Both paths publish the
same three-channel Lab Streaming Layer stream, so the acquisition
and processing modules cannot tell them apart. Offline debugging,
regression testing, and pipeline development happen in mock mode
without a device attached.

\textbf{Signal extraction backend.} Two implementations of HR,
RMSSD, and phasic EDA extraction coexist behind an interface. The
default calls NeuroKit2~\citep{makowski2021}; the alternative uses
the official PLUX biosignalsnotebooks package~\citep{plux2020}.
Switching backends requires editing one line of configuration.
Both feed identical downstream math. Section~\ref{sec:backends}
covers this at length.

\textbf{VR scene.} The Unity-side contract is fixed and
scene-agnostic. Any scene that implements four incoming commands
(\texttt{start}, \texttt{stop}, \texttt{increase},
\texttt{decrease}) and emits telemetry in the shared JSON envelope
is a valid client. The biofeedback engine holds no scene-specific
code and needs none: the scenario label and the telemetry fields
are learned at runtime from the first packet Unity sends.

\textbf{Recording schema.} The clinical CSV file grows its columns
from the first Unity telemetry packet. If a new phobia adds two
extra fields (say \texttt{spider\_size} and
\texttt{spider\_count}), the recording captures them without any
Python change. If Unity never connects, the header commits with
physiology-only columns and the file still lands on disk.

Each of these decisions is one editable location in the codebase.
Together they mean an experiment that changes phobia, changes
extraction library, or changes replay source can be reconfigured
in minutes, not days.

% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{Physiological signals: what they are}
\label{sec:signals-what}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

The engine ingests two raw sensor channels and derives three
per-tick numbers from them. A short refresher on what those
signals are, and where they come from, keeps the rest of the
section readable.

\textbf{ECG (electrocardiogram)} is the electrical activity of
the heart recorded at the skin surface. Three surface electrodes
in the standard Lead~I arrangement (positive on the left
mid-clavicular line, negative on the right, reference on the
lower ribs) capture the potential difference generated by each
cardiac cycle. Each cycle produces the characteristic P--QRS--T
waveform; only the QRS complex matters for our pipeline, because
its sharp R~peak is the reproducible landmark from which every
downstream heart measure is derived.

\textbf{EDA (electrodermal activity)}, also called skin
conductance or galvanic skin response, measures how easily
electricity flows across the palmar skin. Two silver--silver
chloride electrodes on adjacent fingers of the non-dominant hand
apply a small constant voltage, and the conductance between them
varies with the activity of the eccrine sweat glands. EDA is one
of the most direct real-time indicators of sympathetic arousal,
because eccrine glands on the palmar surface are innervated
solely by the sympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system
\citep{boucsein2012, dawson2016, fowles1980}.

\textbf{HR (heart rate)} is the number of heartbeats per minute,
computed from the intervals between consecutive R~peaks in the
ECG. It reflects the moment-to-moment balance of sympathetic
(accelerating) and parasympathetic (slowing) drive on the heart.

\textbf{HRV (heart-rate variability)} is the beat-to-beat
fluctuation in the RR intervals. We report RMSSD (root mean
square of successive differences), a time-domain metric that is
dominated by parasympathetic influence and that decreases during
acute stress \citep{taskforce1996, shaffer2017, thayer2012,
kim2018}.

% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{Hardware and materials}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

Each participant wears a wireless biosignalsplux hub on a chest
band, manufactured by PLUX Wireless Biosignals (Lisbon, Portugal).
Two sensor cables plug into the hub: a three-lead ECG cable in
the standard Lead~I configuration, and a two-pad electrodermal
cable on the non-dominant hand. The hub samples both channels
synchronously at 200~Hz with 16-bit ADC resolution over a
three-volt reference. 200~Hz is well above the Nyquist rate for
ECG (a QRS complex has energy up to about 40~Hz) and comfortably
above the requirements for EDA (a phasic skin conductance
response evolves over seconds).

Bluetooth Low Energy carries the data from the hub to a Windows
workstation. The vendor software (OpenSignals) publishes the
samples over the Lab Streaming
Layer~\citep{kothe2019}\footnote{Lab Streaming Layer is
open-source and available at \url{https://github.com/sccn/labstreaminglayer}.},
and any process running locally can subscribe. Our engine is one
such subscriber. One subtlety is worth flagging up front: the
OpenSignals bridge publishes already-converted physical units
(microsiemens for skin conductance, millivolts for ECG), not raw
ADC integers as commonly assumed. Vendor documentation is silent
on the point, and we spent several days chasing a ``constant 25
microsiemens'' reading that turned out to be the correctly
converted value of a saturated ADC. A configuration flag now
switches between the two conventions so the same code handles
archived recordings that carry raw values.

Immersion runs on a Meta Quest headset tethered to the workstation
over Quest Link. Unity 2022 LTS~\citep{unity2024} renders the
scene and speaks to the biofeedback engine through local UDP
sockets, not through the Quest Link tether. Nothing clinically
important depends on the specific headset model; the setup would
work with any Unity-compatible head-mounted display.

% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{Tools and alternatives}
\label{sec:tools}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

Each component of the framework has established alternatives, and
in most cases the choice was based on prior lab experience or on
a specific technical property. Table~\ref{tab:tools} lists the
tool actually used for each role, the best-known alternatives
considered, and the reason for the choice. The intent is
transparency rather than advocacy: any of the alternatives could
be dropped in with local code changes.

