Two additional wearable stress-recognition benchmarks are worth
naming in this context, because they anchor the design space even
though we do not use them directly. WESAD~\citep{schmidt2018}
provides a public multimodal dataset of chest and wrist
physiology under laboratory stress conditions, and it is the
common comparator when validating classifier variants.
\citet{alberdi2016} survey office-environment stress recognition
across the multimodal literature, giving a picture of which
signal combinations have historically worked well and under what
conditions.

% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{System architecture}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

\subsubsection{Component overview}

The server is a Python 3.10 process. It is split into modules with
one job each, and communication between them happens through
explicit function calls; there is no shared mutable state to
reason about.

On the input side, a data-source module subscribes to the Lab
Streaming Layer feed, reads the channel labels to learn which
channel carries which sensor, and applies conversion formulas
when needed. Sanity bounds reject samples outside plausible
physiological ranges, holding the last valid value in their place
so downstream code always sees a plausible number.

The middle of the pipeline is the signal-processing module. It
maintains the rolling window that feeds the phasic decomposition,
accumulates the baseline buffer during the calibration phase, and
publishes cleaned per-tick values. A fusion module then computes
per-signal deltas against the frozen baseline, standardises them,
combines them with the Moldoveanu weights, and classifies the
result into three states.

Output splits three ways. One module broadcasts every tick on the
Lab Streaming Layer so the dashboard can subscribe. A second sends
short UDP commands to Unity. A third listens for JSON telemetry
packets coming back from Unity and hands them to the recorder.

Persistence lives in a session-manager module. Each session ends
with three files on disk: a clinical record, a forensic per-tick
trace, and a JSON metadata file that carries intake, baseline, and
the locked classification thresholds.

Not one of these modules knows which scenario is running. The
scenario label is learned at runtime from the first telemetry
packet Unity sends, and it determines which extra columns get
added to the clinical record.


