The design intent behind Figure~\ref{fig:contract} is that the
recording captures whatever any scene chooses to emit, without
knowing in advance what those fields are. The first telemetry
packet of a session fixes the column set, and every subsequent
packet is a value fill for those columns.
Section~\ref{sec:session-record} covers the recording detail.

\subsubsection{The contract between server and Unity}

Two directions, asymmetric on purpose.

Commands from the server go out as short UTF-8 strings, one per
UDP datagram. Four values are legal: \texttt{start} marks the
beginning of the live phase, \texttt{stop} marks the end,
\texttt{increase} means the participant is calm and the scene may
escalate, \texttt{decrease} means the participant is stressed and
the scene should back off. Unity is free to interpret each command
however the loaded scene requires. In the balloon-flight scene,
\texttt{increase} raises altitude; in a public-speaking scene,
\texttt{increase} might bring a new person into the room. The
engine does not care.

Telemetry from Unity uses one shape for every scene:

\begin{quote}\texttt{\{"scenario": "<name>", "data": \{<field>: <value>, \dots\}\}}\end{quote}

Server treats the packet as opaque. The scenario label labels the
scene; the fields inside \texttt{data} are recorded verbatim.
Nothing on the server declares which fields are legal or expected.
As a consequence, adding a fourth phobia requires no biofeedback
change at all.

% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{Signal acquisition and conditioning}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

Sensor packets arrive on the LSL feed and pass through three
checks before any pipeline math touches them.

Range first. Values outside plausible physiological limits are
rejected as sensor artifacts. Skin conductance is clipped to the
zero to eighty microsiemens interval; ECG to a few millivolts
either side of zero; heart rate to the thirty to two hundred and
twenty beats-per-minute band; RMSSD to the five to five-hundred
millisecond band. Rejected samples never enter the baseline
buffer, and the previous valid value is held so downstream code
always sees something plausible.

Then a disconnect check. If the variance of a signal collapses
below a small threshold for more than fifteen seconds, the code
assumes an electrode has come loose or a cable has been unplugged
and issues a warning to the operator console. It does not abort.
The warning is a hint, not a hard failure.

Underneath all of this sits a three-tier sampling-rate cascade.
The device runs at 200~Hz. The internal processing tick runs at
10~Hz, because the physiology does not change faster than that
and both HR and HRV have their own trailing-window estimators
underneath. The Unity command channel is throttled to at most one
command per second, because a rapid succession of balloon or
audience changes looks unnatural inside VR. Three rates,
decoupled, each configurable in one place.

Two extraction backends live in the code in parallel.
NeuroKit2~\citep{makowski2021}\footnote{NeuroKit2 source and
documentation at \url{https://github.com/neuropsychology/NeuroKit}.}
is the default and matches the reference implementation from the
lab's prior work. biosignalsnotebooks~\citep{plux2020} (the
official PLUX package) is the alternative and is available at
\url{https://github.com/pluxbiosignals/biosignalsnotebooks}. Both
feed identical downstream math. This redundancy has a purpose:
any recorded session can be rerun through both chains offline
and their agreement quantified as a Pearson correlation on HR,
RMSSD, and the composite stress index. That check appears in the
results section as evidence that the choice of extraction library
does not drive the finding.

\subsubsection{Heart rate and heart-rate variability}
\label{sec:hr-hrv}

Cleaning happens first, always. Raw ECG passes through a high-pass
filter at 0.5~Hz to remove baseline drift and a notch filter at
50~Hz to remove mains hum (60~Hz in the United States). Cleaning
is not optional: the Pan and Tompkins
detector~\citep{pantompkins1985} returns zero peaks on raw
mains-contaminated ECG, and even the hybrid NeuroKit2 detector
fares only marginally better.

Peak detection follows. Both backends produce a series of R-peak
indices. NeuroKit2 combines a hybrid detection method with Kubios
artifact correction on top~\citep{lipponen2019}, and the correction
interpolates missed beats before they can distort the RMSSD
calculation. biosignalsnotebooks uses the classic Pan-Tompkins
detector directly, with no interpolation step.

From the R-peaks, per-window heart rate is the reciprocal of the
mean inter-peak interval, expressed in beats per minute:

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{HR} \;=\; \frac{60 \cdot f_{s}}{\overline{\Delta R}}
\qquad \text{[beats per minute]}
\end{equation}

Here $\Delta R = R_{i+1} - R_{i}$ is the sample-index difference
between successive R-peaks and $f_{s}$ is the ECG sampling rate.
Heart rate is recomputed every 0.5~seconds over a trailing window
of thirty seconds, and it is held with zero-order hold between
recomputes.

Heart-rate variability, as reported in this paper, is the root
mean square of successive differences between consecutive RR
intervals. RMSSD is the most widely used time-domain HRV metric
in short recordings and has a large validation literature as an
acute-stress marker~\citep{thayer2012, kim2018}:

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{RMSSD} \;=\; \sqrt{\frac{1}{N-1}
\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \bigl( \mathrm{RR}_{i+1} - \mathrm{RR}_{i} \bigr)^{2}}
\qquad \text{[milliseconds]}
\end{equation}

Individual intervals themselves are
$\mathrm{RR}_{i} = 1000 \cdot \Delta R_{i} / f_{s}$
in milliseconds. RMSSD is recomputed on the same 0.5-second cadence
as HR, but over a longer trailing window of sixty seconds.

Sixty seconds is deliberate, and it cites two bodies of
literature. The Task Force 1996 guidelines~\citep{taskforce1996}
recommend 300~seconds for stable short-term HRV. Ultra-short-term
literature since then , \citet{munoz2015}, \citet{shaffer2017}
, has established 60~seconds as the practical minimum where the
standard deviation of the RMSSD estimator drops into a usable
range (about 12~ms at 60~s, versus about 56~ms at 10~s). Anything
shorter and estimator noise begins to overwhelm real
physiological signal.

Malik and colleagues~\citep{taskforce1996} contribute the RMSSD
artifact gate. After Kubios interpolation, each RR interval is
compared to its predecessor. Any pair whose relative change
exceeds 20~percent is removed:

\begin{equation}
\text{keep } \Delta \mathrm{RR}_{i}
\;\text{ if }\; \frac{\lvert \mathrm{RR}_{i+1} -
\mathrm{RR}_{i} \rvert}{\mathrm{RR}_{i}} \le 0.20
\end{equation}

The rationale is straightforward. Ectopic beats that survive
Kubios correction are, by construction, outliers in the
successive-difference series and would inflate RMSSD dramatically.
The alternative backend uses the Lippman--Stein--Lerman ectopy
rule~\citep{lippman1994} instead, which is a slightly different
formulation of the same idea (removing pairs rather than single
beats).

\subsubsection{Electrodermal activity}
\label{sec:eda}

Skin conductance is the sum of two components with very different
timescales. A tonic level drifts over minutes and reflects
electrode hydration, ambient temperature, and general arousal
baseline. Faster, seconds-scale bumps called skin conductance
responses sit on top of the tonic, one per sympathetic event. Only
the phasic component is useful for real-time stress feedback;
tonic varies too slowly and too often for the wrong reasons
\citep{boucsein2012, dawson2016, fowles1980}.

\citet{greco2016} formulated the cvxEDA algorithm to recover the
two components jointly. It solves an inverse problem under a
sparsity prior on the phasic driver:

\begin{equation}
\min_{\mathbf{t}, \mathbf{p}, \mathbf{q}} \;
\frac{1}{2} \lVert \mathbf{y} - \mathbf{t} - \mathbf{p} -
\mathbf{L}\mathbf{q} \rVert_{2}^{2}
+ \alpha \lVert \mathbf{A}\mathbf{p} \rVert_{1}
+ \frac{\gamma}{2} \lVert \mathbf{B}\mathbf{t} \rVert_{2}^{2}
\end{equation}

Observed skin conductance is $\mathbf{y}$, tonic is $\mathbf{t}$,
phasic driver is $\mathbf{p}$, and $\mathbf{q}$ absorbs unmodelled
dynamics. Full derivation appears in \citet{greco2016}; we call
the implementation wrapped by NeuroKit2.

Every half second, cvxEDA runs on the most recent 60~seconds of
raw EDA, resampled to 10~Hz. The last sample of the recovered
phasic component becomes the current phasic value. One safeguard
matters: any phasic reading whose absolute magnitude exceeds
1~microsiemens is discarded as edge-of-window ringing from the
resampler. Real phasic responses rarely exceed that magnitude and
never appear as isolated one-sample spikes.

The alternative backend has no cvxEDA. It offers a simpler recipe
from the biosignalsnotebooks EDA tutorial: subtract from the raw
signal its low-pass filtered version, with the cutoff at the
canonical 0.05~Hz boundary. That mode is available for comparison;
the default remains cvxEDA.

% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{Personal calibration}
\label{sec:baseline}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

Every session opens with 120~seconds of baseline. Participant sits
quietly with sensors already on and the VR scene not yet started.
Three things happen during and after this window.

Averages come first. Cleaned EDA, HR, and RMSSD across the
baseline are averaged after a three-sigma outlier filter removes
movement artifacts:

\begin{equation}
\mu_{x} \;=\; \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} x_{i}
\qquad \text{for } x \in \{\mathrm{EDA},\ \mathrm{HR},\ \mathrm{RMSSD}\}
\end{equation}

Summation restricts to samples within three standard deviations of
the crude sample mean. Rejected-sample counts are logged for later
review.

Standard deviations come next, one per delta signal. Each will
divide the corresponding live delta during standardisation in the
live phase, so a low-quality baseline directly compresses the
dynamic range of the classifier.

Sigma floors sit on top. If a measured sigma falls below a
physiological minimum, it is clamped upward to that floor and the
console prints a warning. There are three floors: two percentage
points on heart-rate deviation, five percentage points on RMSSD
deviation, and 0.02~microsiemens on phasic EDA. Purpose is to
stop a flat baseline , from an unusually still participant, or
from a technical acquisition issue , from producing enormous
standardised scores on trivial live deviations. Values were chosen
from the physiological ranges reported in \citet{taskforce1996},
\citet{shaffer2017}, and \citet{boucsein2012}. They are
conservative rather than tight.

Baseline collection is the most fragile part of the whole
procedure. Nervous participants have already-elevated baselines,
and every subsequent live tick then looks calm by comparison.
Operator protocol includes a short briefing before baseline begins
and continuous monitoring of the trace to catch fidgeting. In
practice, any baseline where a sigma floor had to engage more than
once is discarded and the session is restarted.

% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
\subsection{Stress index calculation}
% ,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,

Three physiological channels reduce to one scalar via the
weighted-composite formulation of \citet{moldoveanu2023}. The
choice to combine EDA, HR, and HRV into a single scalar is
standard practice in the stress-recognition literature
\citep{alberdi2016, schmidt2018, kreibig2010}.

\subsubsection{Per-signal deltas}

Each channel first becomes a delta against the frozen personal
baseline:

\begin{align}
\Delta_{\mathrm{EDA}} &= \mathrm{EDA}_{\mathrm{phasic}}
&& \text{[microsiemens]} \\[2pt]
\Delta_{\mathrm{HR}} &= \frac{\mathrm{HR}_{\mathrm{live}} - \mu_{\mathrm{HR}}}
{\mu_{\mathrm{HR}}} \cdot 100 && \text{[percent]} \\[2pt]
\Delta_{\mathrm{HRV}} &= \frac{\mu_{\mathrm{HRV}} - \mathrm{HRV}_{\mathrm{live}}}
{\mu_{\mathrm{HRV}}} \cdot 100 && \text{[percent, inverted]}
\end{align}

Sign convention: every delta increases during stress. EDA rises
with sympathetic arousal, so its phasic value enters as-is. Heart
rate rises for the same reason, and the percentage change is
signed positive for stress. HRV works the other way.
Parasympathetic withdrawal during stress shortens vagally
mediated beat-to-beat variability, so a decrease in RMSSD
contributes positively to the composite. The inversion is
standard in the psychophysiology literature
\citep{kim2018, kreibig2010, boucsein2012, thayer2012}.

\subsubsection{Standardisation}

Each delta is divided by its own baseline standard deviation:

\begin{equation}
z_{x} \;=\; \frac{\Delta_{x} - \mu_{\Delta_{x}}}{\sigma_{\Delta_{x}}}
\qquad \text{for } x \in \{\mathrm{EDA},\ \mathrm{HR},\ \mathrm{HRV}\}
\end{equation}

Why standardise? The three raw deltas have wildly different
natural magnitudes. Skin conductance moves in tenths of
microsiemens; heart rate in single-digit percentages; RMSSD in
tens of percent. Without standardisation the signal with the
largest raw span would dominate the composite by scale alone,
regardless of how informative it was. Standardisation also makes
cross-participant comparisons meaningful, because each score
lands in units of the participant's own baseline variability.

\subsubsection{Weighted composite}

The three standardised deltas combine with fixed weights drawn
from \citet{moldoveanu2023}:

\begin{equation}
S_{\mathrm{instant}} \;=\; 0.5 \cdot z_{\mathrm{EDA}}
+ 0.3 \cdot z_{\mathrm{HRV}}
+ 0.2 \cdot z_{\mathrm{HR}}
\end{equation}

Weights reflect established knowledge about the specificity of
each signal to sympathetic arousal on short timescales. Skin
conductance is the most direct real-time sympathetic indicator
and gets the largest share. HRV integrates parasympathetic
withdrawal over longer windows and gets the next. Raw heart rate
is non-specific , it moves for physical activity as much as for
emotional arousal , so it must be weighted down or it swamps
the composite during normal movement
\citep{healey2005, sano2013}.

Using published weights instead of tuning our own keeps the
paper's contribution on the framework rather than on rediscovering
a fusion rule.

\subsubsection{Smoothing}

The instantaneous composite is smoothed with a trailing rolling
mean before it is used for classification:

\begin{equation}
S_{t} \;=\; \frac{1}{W} \sum_{k=0}^{W-1} S_{\mathrm{instant},\, t-k}
\qquad W = f_{p} \cdot \tau_{\mathrm{smooth}}
\end{equation}

With $\tau_{\mathrm{smooth}} = 3$~seconds and $f_{p} = 10$~Hz,
the window is $W = 30$~samples. Three seconds is a compromise.
Shorter windows let per-tick recompute artifacts from the HR and
RMSSD estimators leak into the classification. Longer windows
attenuate real, fast physiological transients that we want to
catch. The choice was empirical: inspecting live traces from four
early participants, plotting the smoothed trajectory at one,
three, and five seconds, and confirming with the supervisor that
three seconds preserved every clinically meaningful event while
suppressing tick-level noise.


